// PENDING: the first row of the truth table in
// `wiki/spec/language/numeric_values.md` — `-1` of type `i32` compared with
// `10` of type `u64` is LESS — does not compile. It is refused at checking
// with a message the same spec now contradicts:
//
//   applies `Less` to a `i32` value and a `u64` value -- the operands have
//   different integer types and the operation would silently truncate the
//   wider one to the narrower width; convert one with an `as` cast so both
//   are the same type
//
// The spec says builtin fixed-integer comparisons "may differ in width,
// signedness, or both", "require no common operand carrier, implicit cast,
// or representability of either value in the other operand's carrier", and
// that realization "may not substitute an unsigned promotion or reject
// source operands merely because their integer carriers differ". An `as`
// cast is precisely the conversion it says is not required — and for this
// row it is not even available, since neither `-1i32` nor `10u64` is
// representable in the other's carrier.
//
// THE SAME UNIMPLEMENTED RULE HAS TWO DIFFERENT BEHAVIOURS, which is why
// this fixture sits beside `mixed_integer_guard_comparison`. The rejection
// above lives in
// `omega-rust/psi/checking/src/proof_contracts/arithmetic_domains/operand_reports.rs`
// and keys on both operands being DECLARED PLACES with primitive types. A
// length projection has no such type, so `self.i < self.text.len` escapes
// it, is accepted by checking, and is then refused a second time and
// silently in the lowering — `construct_integer_comparison` in
// `values/scalar/boolean_lowering.rs` requires equal carriers — where it
// becomes a Unit-plan omission instead of a diagnostic.
//
// Three orders of work, and the third is the one to be careful about.
// Delete the comparison half of the `operand_reports` rule (it stays
// correct for bitwise, which IS carrier-bound). Let the lowering keep two
// carriers; the checked representation already does, since
// `CheckedBooleanExpression::IntegerComparison` holds two independent
// scalar expressions. Then make Terminal and native compare by
// mathematical value: relaxing only the first two is what would make this
// program compile and answer 71.
//
// Promote to `pass/expressions/` with `expected_native_exit.txt` of 70.
use omega_language_std::console;
use omega::language::core::binding;

data Main {
    console: Binding<Console>;
    signed: i32;
    unsigned: u64;
}

machine Main::main(&mut self) reaches Console {
    self.signed = 0 - 1;
    self.unsigned = 10;
    transition self.signed < self.unsigned {
        true -> correct()
        _ -> promoted()
    }

    // 70 when the compiler keeps `-1` negative, 71 when it promotes the
    // signed value into the unsigned carrier instead.
    state correct(&mut self) { self.console.exit_process(70); }
    state promoted(&mut self) { self.console.exit_process(71); }
}
